WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.600
I think it's just been a
very interesting time, right?

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.670
I think so much has
happened in this two years.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:12.670
Technology, Zooms, George
Floyd, I mean it's a lot, right?

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.600
For me as a creative, all
those things have influenced

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.690
and touched me, and I
think it's just great

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.280
that we can start to see people again

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:25.620
I could do this with you.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.390
In this conversation, you're going to hear

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:30.960
about dressing Oscar winners,
the return of the catwalk,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.660
and whether working from
home killed the suit.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.590
If you've been dressing for
work from just the waist up,

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:52.970
you've definitely missed this.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:57.970
The crowds, a touch of glamor,
dressing up and heading out.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:02.030
He has dressed heads of state, royalty,

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.810
and Hollywood stars on and off screen.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.000
He describes a suit as a coat of armor,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.600
and believes wearing
one boosts confidence.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.470
He is a tailor, after all.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.160
Now he's back for London Fashion Week.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.720
Hello, and welcome.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.730
My guest today is the fashion designer

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.700
and Savile Row powerhouse, Ozwald Boateng.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:21.533
Ozwald,

00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:22.769
great to have you with us.
Thank you,

00:01:22.769 --> 00:01:23.940
thank you very much.
I have to say,

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.110
the best dressed man I think
I've had in the studio.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:28.137
Thank you, I'm gonna take that.

00:01:28.137 --> 00:01:30.840
But definitely gonna absorb
that, yeah, absolutely,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:31.673
thank you.
You should,

00:01:31.673 --> 00:01:32.506
you have not let us down.

00:01:32.506 --> 00:01:33.339
It's beautiful.

00:01:33.339 --> 00:01:34.172
What is this?

00:01:34.172 --> 00:01:35.940
This is--
So this is actually

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.020
a flannel, very English fabric,

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:44.020
but the design is based on a
fabric called the kente cloth,

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:48.350
which is a fabric that
is born out of Ghana,

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:50.250
and specifically by the tribe,

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:55.250
the Ashanti who you had
specifically young men who weave it,

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:56.472
hand-weave it.

00:01:56.472 --> 00:01:57.305
Men weave it?

00:01:57.305 --> 00:01:59.080
Yeah, men, exactly.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.530
There's a whole superstition
about women weaving,

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:02.363
and so there's--
Ah okay, there's always

00:02:02.363 --> 00:02:04.890
a superstition with women.
There's, exactly, so,

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:08.210
and the fabric is always
made for effectively chiefs,

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.060
which are effectively kings.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:13.533
So in a way, it's like a, the
kente cloth's like a coat of,

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.748
your tribe, exactly.
Your tribe?

00:02:17.748 --> 00:02:20.590
So I decided to take that

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:24.880
and mix that with English
tradition, flannel.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.830
Take, and usually it's multicolored,

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:30.120
so I took all the colors out
and made it one solid color.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.640
I use fashion and color as a way

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.749
to modernize the traditional
suit, and over time,

00:02:35.749 --> 00:02:39.410
I never recognized that my culture,

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:43.660
my cultural roots were
influencing my choices in color.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.130
So my color palette was very African

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.990
without realizing it was
African, 'cause it just,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.780
the color made sense to me.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.360
I always went for color that
had a richness and depth,

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.180
because if you have a richness
and depth in the color,

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.153
it means more people can wear it.

00:02:59.153 --> 00:03:01.770
They will say I, I'd hear many times

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.423
that I can't wear that color
because it doesn't work,

00:03:04.423 --> 00:03:06.690
and I'd say, well actually it's,

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:07.950
that's not really what it is.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.400
Actually, it's at what shade?

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.480
There's like 1,000
different versions of blue,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.340
and it's endless.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:15.730
So it's all about the right shade,

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.980
so I developed my own
sort of color palette

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.610
or shades which meant
many people could wear,

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:24.830
and then it evolved from
there and then at a point,

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:27.570
when I recognized that my
color palette was very based

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.620
in my African heritage,

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:34.170
that's when I started bringing
more of my understanding

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.640
in terms of textile into my work.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.040
London Fashion Week is back.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.720
There's been a break,
of course, the pandemic,

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.810
and this is your first time showing in...

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:43.521
12 years, can you imagine?

00:03:43.521 --> 00:03:44.354
It's been a while.
A while.

00:03:44.354 --> 00:03:47.230
So are we seeing things
like this in the collection?

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.617
You'll see definitely
hints of this, definitely.

00:03:49.617 --> 00:03:51.610
And this has sort of become my,

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:54.230
sort of like my house-designed fabrics,

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:55.360
so you'll definitely see that

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.410
and other variations of it, so.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.580
So the show, can you, I
know you can't tell me

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:03.260
about the designs, but is there a theme?

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.210
What's the thought behind it?

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:10.210
So I did this huge show
in New York in 2019,

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.520
and it was, the show was,
actually was called AI,

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.930
interesting enough,

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.000
but instead of saying
artificial intelligence,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.660
it was more about the individual,

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.670
so I flipped the letters and I
called it Authentic Identity,

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:28.240
so it was more using
the language like this,

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.760
and so the show itself was this bridge

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.310
between African-American
culture and Africa,

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:38.620
and finding a way of
bridging those positions,

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.750
and so the collection really
was a real clarity on that,

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:46.573
so my show that I'm
doing here in London is,

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.020
I'd say, an expansion of that.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.920
Now we're kind of in the
sort of, like I said,

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:57.370
the sort of post-George Floyd time.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.360
I thought it'd be great to
sort of celebrate Black culture

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.577
in this country over the last 50 years.

00:05:04.577 --> 00:05:07.980
And it's interesting, there's
a company out of Nigeria,

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.650
funny enough, a fintech business

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:13.600
which is home-grown out of Nigeria,

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.600
but it's a platform that enables farmers,

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:22.510
they have like nine million
farmers on their platform,

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:25.060
helping them with the
agriculture business,

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.840
and they've actually came
in and effectively supported

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:31.230
and sponsored this
event that I'm creating.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.093
And for me, that's quite
special, 'cause historically,

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:40.320
I would never have thought
that an African tech business,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.830
home-grown, could be in
that position to do that.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:48.580
They're about to list on
the New York Stock Exchange.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:51.490
So the whole thing,
behind the scenes through

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:56.369
to the designs is funded and
the design has that link.

00:05:56.369 --> 00:05:57.864
It has that link, but more importantly,

00:05:57.864 --> 00:06:02.864
it's a very important
point, because in the,

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:06.100
in this shifting change of time,

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.630
it's about having a voice, right?

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:14.550
And a voice as a designer,
a voice in the tech world,

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:18.210
there's all this big debate
about, do you have enough women,

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.270
do you have enough representation

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.640
on your board - Black representation,

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:27.640
all this has been a very
loud shout for the last,

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.360
let's say, 12 months, right?

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.470
And what's beautiful

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:37.470
and I feel quite happy about
is that the traditional rule

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:43.250
of it is, I would have to go elsewhere

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:46.320
to get the support, right?

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.490
And it's great that
it's coming from Africa

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:53.840
to actually support something
here, that's really important.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.840
'Cause it's very easy to lose
track of one, the influence,

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.640
and the creative influence
of the culture here,

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.760
because what makes
actually London special,

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.140
I can say London 'cause I'm a Londoner,

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:12.720
is it's the melting pot
of lots of cultures.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:17.007
And sort of being able to respect

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.950
and receive that I think
is London's future,

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:26.570
and so I feel that this show
will be a very good example,

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:30.090
a catalyst for that realization,

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:32.870
because it's not about
hitting you over the head

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:37.870
with this is who I am,
I'm Black and I'm proud,

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:39.490
it's not about that.

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:42.763
It's more about, this is who I am,

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:48.600
and it's great, and I'm happy
to be here and contribute.

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:51.210
Yeah, so that's really
what the show's about.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:52.740
I wanna get into some of your designs

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.100
and how you come to those,
but I think what's fascinating

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.440
with the pandemic is
fashion will have changed.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:07:59.650
I mean, it's always changing,

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.450
but perhaps this is one of those moments

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:04.690
where we don't go back
potentially, especially men,

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:07.720
to suits, and we were already
moving away from them,

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.810
but now, if you've just
been on a Zoom call--

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:10.643
Look, look,

00:08:10.643 --> 00:08:13.730
I think it's just been a
very interesting time, right?

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:17.820
I think so much has
happened in these two years.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:22.820
Technology, Zooms, George
Floyd, I mean it's a lot, right?

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:28.642
For me as a creative, all of
those things have influenced

00:08:28.642 --> 00:08:31.840
and touched me, and I
think it's just great

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:34.410
that we can start to see people again,

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:36.000
I could do this with you, right?

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.543
And so in terms of what I'm
gonna do with the collection,

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:45.110
there's a strong cultural
African culture aspect

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:48.750
that is probably more visible

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:52.370
in my work now than it
has been previously,

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:55.610
and a real celebration of that,

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:58.000
not that I never did it before,

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.000
but I feel the time, what
we're in today allows

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:06.680
and more importantly, I think
it's important to demonstrate.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:10.050
I still have the traditional values

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:13.560
of my Savile Row background
'cause I love that,

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:14.770
I love the tailoring of that,

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:17.930
and so you see that very
distinctly in the collection,

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:19.970
but also there's an embrace
of this casual wear,

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:22.830
because no one's had to
wear a suit for two years.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:24.250
I think it's quite sad

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:26.390
that we'd seen the decline
of the suit already,

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:29.110
and now perhaps it's maybe gone even more.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:32.150
We've seen in suit sales
and all of that, it's down.

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:33.200
It's really down, yeah,

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:38.200
I mean COVID definitely
kind of compounded that,

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:39.980
and I think prior to that,

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.870
there was just birth of
sort of like casual wear

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:45.930
in a way that it was moving into our,

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:49.230
even our work lines in a significant way,

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:53.940
but where I position myself,

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:57.200
tailoring was never a uniform in my world.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:58.897
Tailoring was a choice.

00:09:58.897 --> 00:10:03.897
So people would come to me for
special occasions, weddings,

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:06.980
award ceremonies,

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:11.980
and so the dynamic
around how I work is more

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:16.630
about pleasing the, finding
the ingredient for the wearer

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:20.240
for whatever the occasion
that you need the suit for,

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:21.530
so it's slightly different.

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:24.210
It is, but I suppose I'm
just asking maybe as well

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:26.840
for your view on whether,
you know, that moment,

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:27.830
I don't know the exact moment,

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:29.810
but people stopped wearing hats.

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:32.220
When we, I wonder if you
think the pandemic is one

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.210
of those moments, not
necessarily just with the suit,

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:38.220
but kind of where we get even
less formal in some ways,

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:39.540
in the way that we dress.
Yeah, yeah, I think, no,

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:41.150
I think for sure,

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:46.150
the pandemic's made us all
not maybe paid the same amount

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:48.870
of attention 'cause you're at home, right?

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:52.150
And you're doing Zoom calls.

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:55.583
But in the same breath,
now it's starting to shift.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:58.830
We all wanna go out, and
now we're starting to think

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:01.580
about what we're wearing,
so that's changed.

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:04.810
What's interesting is is
the purchase on the, online,

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:07.240
on eComm, 'cause apparently
there was a lot of purchases,

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:10.690
but very particular type of
purchases online and then--

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:13.639
Elasticated trousers, if you're
a woman, bigger knickers.

00:11:13.639 --> 00:11:15.358
Exactly, exactly, exactly,

00:11:15.358 --> 00:11:16.875
so that's quite interesting,

00:11:16.875 --> 00:11:21.875
and there's no question that
eComm universe as a consequence

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:28.097
of the pandemic has really
shaped the business,

00:11:28.097 --> 00:11:30.340
and for sure, I've had to react to that.

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:31.380
I think that's interesting as well,

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:32.540
because during lockdown,

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:34.890
did you have to do consultations remotely,

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:37.760
and how did you find moving
the business in that way

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:39.120
and trying to make those connections?

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:40.440
Can you do it?

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:43.020
It was very unusual actually,

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:48.020
and I tried a few consultations on Zoom,

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:50.700
which I did personally, and
that was quite interesting.

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:52.690
I ended up cracking a lot more jokes.

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:54.510
I was more funny on Zoom.

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:57.120
Because the whole thing
seemed so crazy to me,

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:58.830
because you're trying to work out,

00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:03.824
oh could you step further back
from the screen, and it's,

00:12:03.824 --> 00:12:05.042
you're talking to a CEO,
Tilt your head this way.

00:12:05.042 --> 00:12:08.033
you're talking like CEOs
run like $120 billion,

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:11.890
could you move, could you turn around?

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:13.593
I think there is a way to do it.

00:12:14.665 --> 00:12:17.050
I need more time to figure it out.

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:18.810
Well, and the advances in technology,

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:22.840
the idea that you don't need
to measure in person by hand,

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:25.491
it can be done by machines,
what do you make of all of that?

00:12:25.491 --> 00:12:29.450
I think we're still, I think
it's, the tech's out there,

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:33.620
I just, I haven't personally
been able to experience it.

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:35.690
I mean the, ultimately,

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:38.280
the best way we'd do it is if
there's some way you're able

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:42.620
to use that facial
recognition tech on the body.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:46.560
I'm actually of the view
that the future would be,

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:48.270
that everything is made to measure,

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:49.890
and somehow,
Really?

00:12:49.890 --> 00:12:54.517
there is a manufacturing unit that,

00:12:54.517 --> 00:12:56.260
in the way that you're at home

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:59.700
and then you have a request of something,

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:03.300
and it's made for you at the
factory and then sent to you.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:07.040
I know, I feel that it's
possible in the future

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:08.330
for that to happen.

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:10.280
Because it's kind of mad
that it couldn't, right?

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:12.880
That all of it is such an estimate,

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:15.040
the idea that one size

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:18.050
of a type of a size
could fit so many people.

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:18.883
Exactly.

00:13:18.883 --> 00:13:21.570
But it's not just that, because the nature

00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:23.910
of the business is that
I don't know who's going

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:27.310
to purchase it, so if I don't know,

00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:32.310
I have to make 20 variables
of size to fit you, see?

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:38.300
And so what happens is it's
designed to create waste.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:41.470
So if you know the measures,

00:13:41.470 --> 00:13:43.360
then you don't have to make the five sizes

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:44.980
to find you.
So that could be the route

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:48.270
to a greener future, but I
mean big fashion, fashion,

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:49.860
'cause the hugest houses of this,

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.290
and I'm not talking the couture
end, I'm talking the faster

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:54.670
end, they still just don't work

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:55.990
on any way of that level, do they?

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:57.450
The amounts of waste is huge.

00:13:57.450 --> 00:13:59.317
This is a massive issue.
Yeah, yeah, yeah, there's a,

00:13:59.317 --> 00:14:00.510
no, there's, absolutely,

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:03.230
and, but the thing is it's
also a challenge for them,

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:06.823
so, unless you're controlling
your manufacturing,

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:09.760
which a lot of them don't.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:13.010
They may have really good
agreements with manufacturers,

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:16.850
it's a very difficult
one to change outside

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:20.970
of creating a system where
you can bring the garment

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:23.410
that you're no longer working back

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:25.470
where it could be recycled.

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:28.193
I don't see how they're
gonna resolve that issue.

00:14:29.430 --> 00:14:31.240
How many suits do you have?

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:32.073
Good question.

00:14:32.073 --> 00:14:33.030
I recycle.

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:35.120
So interesting enough,

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:37.720
I only have as much as
I need in a given time,

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:40.513
so maybe, it's maybe 20,

00:14:43.853 --> 00:14:44.686
maybe 20?
Is that it?

00:14:44.686 --> 00:14:49.367
Yeah, I wear it and I'll sell it off.

00:14:49.367 --> 00:14:50.690
And I asked that because I'm trying

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:54.170
to think about what you
said about confidence

00:14:54.170 --> 00:14:55.270
and what a suit can do.

00:14:55.270 --> 00:15:00.270
So do you really feel in yourself
when you put on your suit,

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:01.523
what do you feel?

00:15:01.523 --> 00:15:03.180
How does it change you?

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:04.600
Well for me, it's interesting

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:09.070
'cause I've been wearing
suits since I was like 16,

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:11.920
so I have a wholly different
light, it's like putting on,

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:15.190
it's almost like putting on
a tracksuit I would imagine,

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:16.023
for me, right?

00:15:16.023 --> 00:15:18.950
So I don't, I'm not thinking about it.

00:15:18.950 --> 00:15:22.700
But if it's something, I
think, there's two things.

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:27.200
One is the suits and
having a suit made for you,

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:30.810
and then my intention when I create a suit

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:32.290
for an individual.

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:35.040
So my intention is always to

00:15:35.898 --> 00:15:38.123
encourage the wearer

00:15:38.123 --> 00:15:42.050
to be who they are, be in
their truth effectively,

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:45.230
and so, and I like to support that

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:47.300
in the way I cut the suits,

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:49.240
and so once they look at themselves

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:52.210
and they're feeling
better about who they are,

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:53.387
they show more of who they are.

00:15:53.387 --> 00:15:56.900
Suits and clothes are our
armor a lot of the time.

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:59.227
They say how we feel
about ourselves a lot.

00:15:59.227 --> 00:16:01.340
I volunteer with a charity,

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:04.280
a women's charity called Smart Works

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:07.450
where we dress women who are
going for job interviews,

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:08.920
and obviously we're not tailoring it,

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:12.930
it's largely donated clothes,
but when you dress a woman,

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:15.340
when you dress someone,
you help them get dressed,

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:19.090
as an adult so not as a child,
the feeling it gives them,

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:23.100
that effort's gone into their
appearance, is extraordinary.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:26.093
Yes, absolutely, and
it's a very good point.

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:32.680
When I'm dressing a client

00:16:32.713 --> 00:16:34.910
and he's putting the suit on

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:38.710
for the first time after we've
done the fitting process,

00:16:38.710 --> 00:16:40.910
the dressing of that individual,

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:44.110
the experience of putting
the jacket, putting,

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:48.670
helping them put the trouser
on is quite a powerful,

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:51.370
powerful relationship
actually, 'cause effectively,

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:52.430
they undress in front of you,

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:54.800
and then they dress in front of you,

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:57.120
and then that experience
when they look at themselves

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:00.250
in the mirror is quite
something, it's quite special.

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:01.690
Are you on social media?

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:03.180
I'm trying.

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:05.470
My daughter says I'm hopeless.

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:07.510
I'm really trying.

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:09.730
I'm still on the fringes of social media.

00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:10.640
I think you should stay there.

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:11.473
You know?

00:17:12.327 --> 00:17:16.780
I'm sort of, I understand it's,
I need to be more engaged,

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:20.190
but it's interesting
because I think on one hand,

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:21.110
it's fantastic

00:17:21.110 --> 00:17:23.580
because then you get a
chance to really express

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:28.189
and there's no, in a way, middle
man to your communication.

00:17:28.189 --> 00:17:29.930
Also in the same breath,

00:17:29.930 --> 00:17:31.390
anyone could say anything they want.

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:34.064
Truth or not, they can just say,

00:17:34.064 --> 00:17:35.210
this is an issue.
Well also,

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:37.000
there's no gatekeepers
in quite the same way.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:39.730
If you start a business now,
certainly in the fashion world,

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:42.290
you can just get people to
wear your stuff, influencers,

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:43.150
and then it grows.

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:44.960
You don't have to have bricks and mortar

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:46.320
on Savile Row anymore.
No, no, no, exactly,

00:17:46.320 --> 00:17:47.620
and which is, I have to say,

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:49.040
I'm really getting to understand,

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:54.040
because the main thing for
me is the real kind of,

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:57.830
it's always about
connecting with the wearer,

00:17:57.830 --> 00:17:58.860
and I always go back to it.

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:01.130
I'm very spiritual about my work.

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:05.988
And so I like that dynamic

00:18:05.988 --> 00:18:08.263
within every purchase,

00:18:08.263 --> 00:18:11.530
even if they're not even aware
that that's what's happening,

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:12.590
once they put it on

00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:14.620
and they've started feeling
good about themselves.

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:17.990
'Cause I've now been at it long
enough to be able to say it,

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:21.630
'cause probably historically I
wouldn't actually communicate

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:23.250
in that way, I'd say, oh you know,

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:24.530
they enjoy wearing my clothes,

00:18:24.530 --> 00:18:27.050
but it's been a long enough time,

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:29.020
I know that's what it does, right?

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:32.693
And so that for me is
the most important thing,

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:35.780
and how do you communicate that

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:39.480
on social media is interesting.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:40.313
And the fashion world sometimes

00:18:40.313 --> 00:18:44.267
doesn't have the best
reputation, divas and the like,

00:18:44.267 --> 00:18:45.367
and the best behaviors

00:18:45.367 --> 00:18:46.420
and the work behaviors.
Well I have to say though,

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:48.883
I have to say, in the old days of fashion,

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:51.500
you had a lot of that, actually.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:55.920
Lot of egos, huge amount of egos.

00:18:55.920 --> 00:19:00.520
But I'd say probably in
the last five, eight years,

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:02.260
it's really changed a lot.

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:06.890
I remember going to fashion
awards and, of recent,

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:09.960
and everyone going up on stage

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:14.450
and the speeches were
a lot more considered,

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:17.910
not so much about what they've achieved

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.910
or who they are or who
they said they were,

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:24.980
there seems to be a big shift,
and it was needed, because--

00:19:24.980 --> 00:19:26.297
How bad was it before?

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:30.083
At some points it was a
bit, just ridiculous, yeah.

00:19:31.213 --> 00:19:35.432
There's a theater
requirement within fashion,

00:19:35.432 --> 00:19:36.550
because you're a creative.

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:38.110
You're an artist.

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:43.110
So you're entitled to
wanna have purple roses

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:45.440
or whatever it is,
because this is the vision

00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:47.900
that inspires you to create.

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:52.130
But sometimes the performances
went so beyond the realms

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:52.963
of reality

00:19:52.963 --> 00:19:55.094
that they need to,
What's your,

00:19:55.094 --> 00:19:56.406
pruning,
they needed to ground it,

00:19:56.406 --> 00:19:57.239
yeah.
come back.

00:19:57.239 --> 00:19:58.980
They need to be, yeah, step back.

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:02.393
What's your most diva-ish
request, you think?

00:20:05.061 --> 00:20:06.450
That's quite funny, actually.

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:07.283
Go on.

00:20:07.283 --> 00:20:08.440
What have you always gotta have?

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:11.680
Well no, no, actually me, I'm,
actually I think I'm quite,

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:13.250
I'm very clear about what I want,

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:16.740
I'm very detail-oriented
when it comes to my creative,

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:21.089
but probably my only major diva moment

00:20:21.089 --> 00:20:23.670
I would say actually

00:20:23.670 --> 00:20:26.530
is when I was a creative
director at Givenchy.

00:20:26.530 --> 00:20:29.773
I was in the office design room,

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:34.550
and I wasn't getting things
done in the time that I wanted,

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:35.710
and I couldn't understand.

00:20:35.710 --> 00:20:40.710
I was very diplomatic,
and so I went for lunch

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:44.835
with a friend of mine, and then
I was saying that, probably,

00:20:44.835 --> 00:20:47.393
it's not moving quick enough.

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:49.877
And he said, "Yeah, but
they're French, you know,

00:20:49.877 --> 00:20:52.440
"you gotta throw a tantrum."

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:53.847
I said, "What do you mean?"

00:20:53.847 --> 00:20:54.980
"Gotta throw a tantrum?"

00:20:54.980 --> 00:20:55.950
He said, "Yeah, you gotta go in there

00:20:55.950 --> 00:20:57.200
"and you gotta tell 'em what you want,"

00:20:57.200 --> 00:20:59.797
so I go in, I threw a tantrum,

00:20:59.797 --> 00:21:01.379
and I got the stuff done immediately.

00:21:01.379 --> 00:21:03.110
What does that look like,
you throwing a tantrum?

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:03.943
Well I was, I mean it was quite funny

00:21:03.943 --> 00:21:05.933
because it was slightly performance.

00:21:05.933 --> 00:21:08.550
I wanted black tulips.

00:21:08.550 --> 00:21:10.430
It's a whole thing, black tulips,

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:12.850
I want black tulips
everywhere for the stage,

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:13.683
it was a whole thing,

00:21:13.683 --> 00:21:14.727
so.
I want my black tulips

00:21:14.727 --> 00:21:15.870
and I want them now.

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:16.703
Basically.

00:21:16.703 --> 00:21:17.877
Who's the best person you've dressed?

00:21:17.877 --> 00:21:19.223
Are you allowed to say?

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:21.900
Or a memorable moment for you,

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:24.323
with all these stories
and all these people?

00:21:24.323 --> 00:21:25.170
I mean there's so many,

00:21:25.170 --> 00:21:27.430
I mean there's story of Daniel Day-Lewis,

00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:30.550
dressing him for the BAFTAs,

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:34.580
when he won for "Gangs of
New York," I think, yeah,

00:21:34.580 --> 00:21:38.170
and he came to see me
first and I dressed him up

00:21:38.170 --> 00:21:41.610
and that was really
beautiful, 'cause he gets,

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:44.050
he went on stage and he
won the award, he was,

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:45.120
he talked about his tailor

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:47.390
but he didn't know how to
say it, because it was,

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:50.170
because the thing is is when
you have that special moment,

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:52.430
there's a lot of pressure, right?

00:21:52.430 --> 00:21:57.430
And so you, well at least it's what I do,

00:21:57.570 --> 00:22:00.740
I like to make that moment when
I come to dress you a moment

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:04.020
where you don't feel that, so
it takes a lot of that away,

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:08.950
and then when I dressed Jamie Foxx,

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:13.950
when he won his Oscar for
"Ray," so that was great.

00:22:14.610 --> 00:22:15.443
Incredible movie.

00:22:15.443 --> 00:22:18.170
Yeah, great movie, and I've
become very good friends

00:22:18.170 --> 00:22:21.480
with Jamie, and so that was special.

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:23.667
I would say there's been
many special moments.

00:22:23.667 --> 00:22:26.390
And is it special, of course they win

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:29.310
and then you see them,
and they look sharp,

00:22:29.310 --> 00:22:30.830
they look how you've talked about,

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:33.619
but is it the actual
connection that's made

00:22:33.619 --> 00:22:34.930
that's the--
Yeah, that's, for me,

00:22:34.930 --> 00:22:38.403
what's more important is how they feel.

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:43.580
Many, probably I'd say, most of the time

00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:46.852
that I've dressed people,
the usual rule is,

00:22:46.852 --> 00:22:49.460
they're going on a carpet
and you'd say, look,

00:22:49.460 --> 00:22:53.690
make sure you tell them
that I dressed you or,

00:22:53.690 --> 00:22:56.690
and I've always been kind
of the opposite to that.

00:22:56.690 --> 00:23:00.150
I've always said, look, if
they said oh I'll mention,

00:23:00.150 --> 00:23:01.930
I say look, don't worry about that.

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:03.930
If you're feeling good, it will be good.

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:06.780
Thank you so much.

00:23:06.780 --> 00:23:09.720
And thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:12.623
Until we meet again, do
stay safe, and goodbye.

